s=s.replace('These jets are the classes in the finite-dimensional quotients $R^{p_0}/\\mathfrak m_x^kR^{p_0}$, so the germs converge in the analytic Krull topology; closedness gives that the limit germ belongs to $L_x$.','For each $k$, the image of $L_x$ in the finite-dimensional jet space $R^{p_0}/\\mathfrak m_x^kR^{p_0}$ is a linear subspace and hence closed. The limiting jet therefore lies in that image, so the limit germ belongs to $L_x+\\mathfrak m_x^kR^{p_0}$ for every $k$. Krull intersection applied to the finite local module $R^{p_0}/L_x$ gives $\\bigcap_k(L_x+\\mathfrak m_x^kR^{p_0})=L_x$, proving the claimed membership explicitly.')
a=s.index('3.1 For nested chart-open sets');s=s[:a]+r'''3.1 First consider a presentation neighborhood $V$ with its surjective section map and quotient topology. If a small chart polydisc $D\subseteq V$ has a target presentation whose section map is surjective, the restricted images of the finitely many source free generators lift to target free sections on $D$. As in step 2.2, these lifts give a holomorphic matrix on $D$. Restricting source free sections and multiplying by that matrix is continuous for compact-convergence, so passage through the two quotient maps proves continuity of $\Gamma(V,\mathcal F)\to\Gamma(D,\mathcal F)$. For any countable cover $(V_i)$ by such presentation neighborhoods, choose countably many sufficiently small polydiscs $D_{ij\nu}$ covering every overlap $V_i\cap V_j$, with surjective local section maps as in step 2.2. The restrictions from each $V_i,V_j$ into each such polydisc are continuous by the matrix argument. Therefore the compatible families are the intersection of the kernels of the continuous differences into $\Gamma(D_{ij\nu},\mathcal F)$. Those targets are Hausdorff Fréchet spaces by step 2.1, so this intersection is closed in the countable product of the local section spaces. A countable product of Fréchet spaces is Fréchet by enumerating their seminorms and taking componentwise limits; a closed subspace is complete for its restricted metric. Thus the compatible-family space is an actual Fréchet space. Compatibility checked on these overlap covers is full compatibility by the sheaf axiom. [A1, F4, F7, step 2.1, step 2.2, construct]

3.2 To compare the quotient topology on an arbitrary presentation neighborhood $V$ with its topology from local sections, cover $V$ by countably many sufficiently small presentation polydiscs $(D_\nu)$ as in step 2.2. The restriction map from the already Fréchet quotient $\Gamma(V,\mathcal F)$ into the compatible-family space for this cover is continuous by step 3.1. It is a linear bijection by unique sheaf gluing. Both its source and target are Fréchet by step 2.1 and step 3.1, so [F6] makes it a topological isomorphism. In particular, inverse gluing is continuous, and the original arbitrary-$V$ presentation quotient agrees with the compatible-family topology; no unproved identification of these topologies is made. [F4, F6, F7, step 2.1, step 2.2, step 3.1, construct]

3.3 Any two countable presentation covers have a common countable refinement by sufficiently small presentation polydiscs; choose them inside the local vanishing neighborhoods of the finitely many relation kernels involved at each source-target comparison, as in step 2.2. Restriction from either compatible-family space into the refined compatible-family space is continuous by the local matrix argument of step 3.1 and is a bijection by sheaf gluing. All these spaces are Fréchet by step 3.1, so [F6] makes both maps topological isomorphisms. The identity on ordinary sections therefore identifies the two cover topologies. Together with the presentation and chart comparison in step 2.2 and the quotient-to-family comparison in step 3.2, this proves every asserted independence. [A1, F4, F6, F7, step 2.2, step 3.1, step 3.2, construct] ∎
''';p.write_text(s)
